1. Why do we need Ansible Vault?

When we work with AWS using Ansible, we may need sensitive information such as:

AWS Access Key
AWS Secret Access Key
Database passwords
API tokens
SSH passwords
Other application secrets

We should not store these credentials directly in normal Ansible files such as:

inventory.ini
vars.yml
playbook.yml

For example, this is not a good practice:

aws_access_key: AKIAxxxxxxxxxxxx
aws_secret_key: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

If this file is pushed to GitHub, the credentials could be exposed.

Ansible Vault is used to encrypt sensitive information so that the credentials are not stored as plain text.

2. What is Ansible Vault?

Ansible Vault is an Ansible feature used to encrypt sensitive data.

It allows us to store passwords, keys, tokens, and other confidential information in encrypted YAML files.

For example, instead of storing:

aws_access_key: AKIAxxxxxxxx
aws_secret_key: abcdefghxxxxxxxx

as readable text, Vault encrypts the file so that its contents look like encrypted data.

The important point is:

Vault protects the contents of the file.

It does not mean that Ansible automatically knows the password. Ansible needs a Vault password to decrypt the file when it needs to use the secrets.

3. Create a password for Vault

We first need a password that will be used to encrypt and decrypt the Vault file.

=> 

The command used in this lab is:

openssl rand -base64 2048 > vault.pass
What does this command do?

Let's break it down:

a) openssl

openssl is a command-line tool that provides cryptographic functions.

b) rand

rand tells OpenSSL to generate random data.

c) -base64

This converts the generated random data into Base64 format.

d) 2048

This tells OpenSSL to generate 2048 bytes of random data before encoding it.

So this creates a very long random value that can be used as the Vault password.

e) > vault.pass

The > redirects the generated output into a file named:

vault.pass

So instead of displaying the random value on the terminal, it is saved inside the file.

This file contains the password that Ansible Vault will use.

4. Why use a separate vault.pass file?

Instead of typing the Vault password every time, we can tell Ansible where the password is stored:

--vault-password-file vault.pass

This means:

"Ansible, use the password stored inside vault.pass to unlock the Vault."

This is useful for automation because Ansible can use the password file without asking interactively every time.

=> Important security point

vault.pass itself is sensitive.

Do not commit it to GitHub.

For example, add it to .gitignore:

vault.pass

Otherwise, someone who gets your vault.pass may be able to decrypt your Vault-encrypted secrets.

5. Create the Vault file

Now we create a file where we will store our AWS credentials.

The command is:

ansible-vault create group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

=> Let's understand every part.

a) ansible-vault

This is the Ansible Vault command.

It provides operations such as:

create
view
edit
encrypt
decrypt
create
ansible-vault create

b) means:

Create a new encrypted Ansible Vault file.

Ansible will open an editor where we can enter our sensitive information.

c) group_vars/all/pass.yml

This is the file that will contain our sensitive variables.

The structure is:

group_vars/
└── all/
    └── pass.yml

group_vars is an Ansible convention for storing variables related to hosts/groups.

all means the variables can be available to all hosts, depending on how the inventory/playbook is structured.

pass.yml is simply the filename we chose for the password/secret variables.

The filename itself is not mandatory. We could use another name, but pass.yml clearly indicates that it contains sensitive information.

6. What happens when we run the command?

When you execute:

ansible-vault create group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

Ansible will:

Read the Vault password from vault.pass.
Create group_vars/all/pass.yml.
Open the file in an editor.
You enter your sensitive variables.
Ansible encrypts the contents when the file is saved.

For example, inside the editor you might enter:

aws_access_key: YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
aws_secret_key: YOUR_SECRET_KEY

But when you later look at the file normally, you will not see these credentials in plain text.

Instead, the file will contain encrypted content beginning with something similar to:

$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256

followed by encrypted data.

That is the main purpose of Vault.

7. Why is group_vars/all/pass.yml useful?

Ansible automatically looks for variables in specific locations.

A common structure is:

ansible-project/
├── inventory.ini
├── playbook.yml
├── group_vars/
│   └── all/
│       └── pass.yml
└── vault.pass

The idea is:

inventory.ini → defines hosts
playbook.yml → defines what Ansible should do

group_vars/all/pass.yml → stores encrypted sensitive variables
vault.pass → stores the password required to decrypt the Vault

However, vault.pass should remain outside GitHub.

8. What does "Add your AWS credentials using the below vault command" actually mean?

It means we don't put the AWS credentials directly into a normal YAML file.

Instead, we create an encrypted Vault file:

ansible-vault create group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

Then inside that file we enter the required AWS variables.

For example:

aws_access_key: YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
aws_secret_key: YOUR_SECRET_KEY

After saving the file, Ansible encrypts it.

So the overall flow is:

AWS Credentials
      ↓
group_vars/all/pass.yml
      ↓
Ansible Vault encrypts the file
      ↓
Encrypted file
      ↓
GitHub

The actual credentials are therefore not visible in the GitHub repository.
